{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The change is a one-word rename in `gen.py:13` — `value` → `start` in the HR target — documented in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Root cause:** In an Intervals.icu `workout_doc` HR target, the lower-bound key selects the FIT-export mode. `{value, end}` exports as a zone-mapped target, so the watch clamps each corridor to its own zone bands (and follows the watch's stale auto-detected LTHR of 168). `{start, end}` exports as an Instant-HR corridor: Intervals converts the percentages to bpm using the LTHR it stores (172), and the watch shows that raw range.\n\n**Why I'm confident:** the failure history in `readback/watch-notes.md` matches this schema exactly — the failed `bpm` and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` variants are the two other shapes that misroute or get dropped, and a second source (an [Intervals.icu forum readback](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-create-or-update-workout-without-using-description-text-syntax/124215)) confirms native targets use `start`/`end`. The writeup came from a [field report of this exact bug](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts).\n\n**Verified locally:** `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now emits the same body as `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` with only `value`→`start` differing; the plan percentages × 172 reproduce the watch's expected columns (117–129 / 151–160 / 129–141 / 112–124). End-to-end watch confirmation still needs a real push + Garmin sync, which I didn't do — no external calls were made.\n\nOne adjacent issue left out of scope, noted in ANSWER.md: with no top-level `description`, steps stay invisible in the Intervals web UI (a display concern, unrelated to the watch clamping)."}
